    "body": "## Problem\r\n\r\nThe ui2 ruler no longer is a (left-aligned) box of fixed width, but the remnants of ui1 still unnecessarily constrain the ruler to a maximum width. This width is configured by reusing the item group syntax in an inconsistent way, which causes a number of surprising edge-cases. The inconsistencies have become more apparent when the C implementation of the statusline was replaced with a default expression in 0.12, since it reuses the ruler as an expression instead of pre-rendered. Furthermore, `v:echospace` is still set according to the old rules.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\nRemove arbitrary constraints and process item groups in `rulerformat` the same as in all the other statusline-style options. The current fallback ruler expression in the default `statusline` (`%-14.(%l,%c%V%)\\ %P`) now actually works for `rulerformat` itself, even with different `minwid` values and both with or without the period.\r\n\r\nMake ui2 itself responsible of setting `v:echospace` according to its layout of the last line. It must be updated whenever the ruler changes in size, e.g. when `rulerformat=%l` and the cursor moves from line 9 to line 10.\r\n\r\nI couldn't find any internal state that could simply be exposed as `v:echospace`. In particular, `messages.cmd.last_col` is related, but different: as far as I can tell, it tracks the current space allocated for a currently displayed message, which can fluctuate e.g. when search count or `showcmd` (dis)appear. But this is uninteresting for the purposes of `v:echospace`, which tracks the space available for newly displayed messages, and those write over any search count and are delayed until `showcmd` disappears again (this is another departure from ui1, where space is reserved for `showcmd` outside of `v:echospace`). I documented my current understanding of the cropping behaviour in the form of unit tests, except for an `echo` inside a multi-line/control-flow command, which I found difficult to test.\r\n\r\nFix #40247",
